@page "/learn/cacheview"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Caching part of a page · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Caching part of a page</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">One panel on a page runs a slow report query on every view. How do you cache just that panel?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Rendering · Static SSR · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>&lt;CacheView&gt;</code> saves the finished HTML for part of a page.
        .NET 10 could already cache data with <code>IMemoryCache</code>, or a whole page with output caching. It
        couldn't cache the HTML of just one part. With CacheView, the component inside isn't even created when its
        HTML is already cached.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Stock by category</i> page. Its report takes 1.5 seconds, so it caches its data for 10
        seconds with <code>IMemoryCache</code>, the usual .NET 10 approach:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 report's own cache</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="err">report = (await Cache.GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report", async entry =&gt;</span>
{
    entry.AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);
    <span class="err">return new Report(await StockQuery.RunAsync(DbFactory), DateTime.Now);</span>
}))!;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In the demo, <b>Page rendered at</b> shows when the page was built. <b>Figures calculated at</b> shows when
        the report last ran.
    </p>
    <p>
        The <b>Five visitors at once</b> button waits for the cached figures to expire, then sends five requests at
        the same moment, like a busy morning. The queries run on the server, where you can't see them, so it then
        shows how many those five ran.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The report's own cache, with five visitors at once" Url="demo/cacheview/stock/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: IMemoryCache in the component" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Five visitors at once</b>. You'll see <b>5 visitors, 5 queries ran</b>: every visitor ran the
            slow report.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Everyone runs the query at once</h2>
    <p>Caching the data works while it's in the cache. The trouble starts when it expires:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Five visitors arrive just after the entry expired</span>
        <pre class="snip">visitor 1  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 2  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 3  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 4  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
visitor 5  GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report")  <span class="err">miss → runs the query</span>
<span class="dim">5 × 1.5 s of database work, for one report</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        With <code>IMemoryCache.GetOrCreateAsync</code>, nobody waits for the first visitor's query. Every visitor who
        finds the cache empty runs the query themselves. And even when the data is cached, the component is still created and draws its
        table again on every request. The caching code also lives inside the component: the key, the expiry and the
        <code>@@inject</code>.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Wrap it in a CacheView</h2>
    <p>Take the caching code out of the report, and wrap the report where the page uses it.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;CacheView ExpiresAfter="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10)"&gt;</span>
    &lt;StockSummary /&gt;
<span class="hl">&lt;/CacheView&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The first time, Blazor renders <code>StockSummary</code> and saves the HTML. Anyone arriving at the same
        moment waits for that one render and gets the same HTML. After that, Blazor sends the saved HTML and doesn't
        create <code>StockSummary</code> at all. The rest of the page still renders every time. There's no setup in
        <code>Program.cs</code>: unlike <code>IMemoryCache</code>, which needs <code>AddMemoryCache()</code>, CacheView
        stores its entries in memory by default.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same page, with CacheView" Url="demo/cacheview/stock"
                Label=".NET 11: CacheView">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Five visitors at once</b>. You'll see <b>5 visitors, 1 query ran</b>: the other four waited for
            it.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        <code>StockSummaryNet10</code> is the .NET 10 report, with its own caching code. <code>StockSummary</code>
        is the same report with that code removed. Both use a shared <code>StockTable</code> to draw the table.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Caching/StockSummaryNet10.razor" Feature="cacheview">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's report. It picks its own key, <code>"stock-report"</code>, and its own expiry. Every visitor who
            finds the cache empty runs the query.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Caching/StockReport.razor" Feature="cacheview">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page, with a CacheView around <code>StockSummary</code>. <code>StockSummary</code> has no caching
            code now: it just runs the query in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Cache the data, or the whole page</h2>
    <p>.NET 10 had no way to cache the HTML of part of a page. Here are the two usual workarounds:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to cache the report in .NET 10, and what a .NET 10 build did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@inject IMemoryCache Cache
<span class="err">Cache.GetOrCreateAsync("stock-report", ...)</span></pre>
                <span class="why">fast on hits, but needs <code>AddMemoryCache()</code>, and the component is still created on every request</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ five visitors who miss together run the query five times</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/stock"
<span class="err">@@attribute [OutputCache(Duration = 10)]</span></pre>
                <span class="why">fast, but all or nothing: the page's <b>Page rendered at</b> time freezes too</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ add a form to the page and output caching quietly turns off: every view takes 1.5 s, with no warning</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>HybridCache</code> (.NET 9 and later, in its own package) would stop the five queries: it makes the
        other visitors wait for the first. But it's still a data cache. The component is still created and draws its
        table on every request, and the caching code still lives inside it. CacheView fixes all of that from the
        page.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Cache the HTML, not the data</h2>
    <p>
        .NET 11 caches the finished HTML. When it's cached, the component inside is skipped completely, which no
        data cache can do. And the page decides what to cache, not the component.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Every visitor gets the same HTML.</b> Each CacheView is cached once, for everyone (once per <code>CacheKey</code> value, if you set one). It ignores the user, query string and route, so one user's data would be shown to the next. Add <code>VaryByUser</code>, <code>VaryByQuery</code>, <code>VaryByRoute</code> or <code>VaryByCulture</code> for anything that differs per visitor.</li>
        <li><b>Entries last 30 seconds by default.</b> Set <code>ExpiresAfter</code> or <code>ExpiresOn</code> to change that. With <code>ExpiresSliding</code> alone, the entry still expires after 30 seconds, so pair it with one of the other two.</li>
        <li><b>Keep one small part fresh.</b> Mark a component <code>[CacheBehavior(CacheBehavior.Rerender)]</code> and it renders on every request, even when the rest is cached. The dashboard uses one to show how old its figures are.</li>
        <li><b>Only normal page loads on static pages are cached.</b> A form submission always renders fresh. So does a CacheView inside a component marked <code>[StreamRendering]</code>. Don't use it in an interactive part of the page.</li>
        <li><b>Already using <code>HybridCache</code>?</b> If one is registered, CacheView stores its entries there.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
